    "body": "## Problem\n\nAn error in a Lua autocmd callback is reported, but does not abort the command that triggered it, unlike a Vimscript `:throw`. `error()` in a `BufWritePre` callback does not prevent `:write` for example.\n\nFixes #36153\n\n## Solution\n\nSet `force_abort` when the Lua callback emits an error, so that `aborting()` returns true for the caller. This is the approach @ashab-k suggested in the issue.\n\n`try`/`catch` is unaffected. Note that the bug only shows up for typed or mapped commands: API calls (`nvim_command()` for example) run inside an implicit `:try`, which already aborts, so the test uses `feed()`.\n\nNote that this should make any Lua callback error behave like an uncaught `:throw`, so remaining handlers for the event and later commands are skipped as well.  Happy to try and narrow it if that's too broad.\n\nAI-assisted.",
